export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"cadcraft","addon":"pid","name":"P&ID","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"pid-start","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: getting started","summary":"What the P&ID add-on does in CadCraft, how to switch it on, and how a diagram is kept in an ordinary DWG or DXF file.","section":"start","order":1,"keywords":["p&id","pid","process","instrumentation","diagram","piping and instrumentation","tag","line number","instrument","equipment","line list","add-on"],"headings":[{"id":"what-it-is","text":"What it is","level":2},{"id":"your-first-diagram","text":"Your first diagram","level":2},{"id":"files-open-anywhere","text":"Files open anywhere","level":2},{"id":"units","text":"Units","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-is\"\u003EWhat it is\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EP&amp;ID is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It draws process and instrumentation diagrams: equipment, valves, instruments and the lines between them, with a tag on each item and a number on each line. From the finished drawing it makes the lists a diagram is read for (line list, equipment list, instrument index, valve list) and checks the drawing for the mistakes that are easy to miss.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIt is off until you switch it on: \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EGeneral\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EAdd-ons\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP&amp;ID\u003C/span\u003E. Its tools are in the \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP&amp;ID\u003C/span\u003E section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP&amp;ID\u003C/span\u003E, as a group at the end of the toolbar.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EA drafting aid, not an engineering approval.\u003C/strong\u003E The symbols are drawn by CadCraft after the common ideas of the standards named in &quot;P&amp;ID: symbols, tags and equipment&quot;; the line numbers, tags and checks follow the usual habits of these diagrams, not any company&#039;s or standard&#039;s rules. A person who is responsible for the plant still decides what the diagram has to say.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"your-first-diagram\"\u003EYour first diagram\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E to choose a symbol (first its kind, then the symbol), and click where it goes. Equipment asks for its tag; the next free one is offered (\u003Ccode\u003EP-101\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003ET-101\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLINE\u003C/code\u003E, type \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E for the service, size and spec (\u003Ccode\u003EPW\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EA1A\u003C/code\u003E), and click the corners of a process line. Start and finish on a nozzle: the click lands on it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPut a valve on the line: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E, a valve, and click on the line. The line is cut either side of the valve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPlace an instrument, type its letters (\u003Ccode\u003EFIC\u003C/code\u003E) and it takes the next loop number (\u003Ccode\u003EFIC-101\u003C/code\u003E). Draw a signal line to it: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLINE\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E for a signal style.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDCHECK\u003C/code\u003E and go to each finding. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLIST\u003C/code\u003E and click where a list goes on the sheet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"files-open-anywhere\"\u003EFiles open anywhere\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA symbol, a line and a list on the sheet are each saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name \u003Ccode\u003ECC_PID\u003C/code\u003E. A diagram saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as drawn, and in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EConnections are never stored: CadCraft works them out from where things touch, so moving a pump with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EMOVE\u003C/code\u003E keeps the diagram true.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"units\"\u003EUnits\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ESymbols are 4 in (100 mm in a metric drawing) by default; change it in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP&amp;ID\u003C/span\u003E. Sizes in line numbers are text: type what your drawings say (\u003Ccode\u003E2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EDN50\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What it is P&ID is an add-on for CadCraft that comes with it and is free. It draws process and instrumentation diagrams: equipment, valves, instruments and the lines between them, with a tag on each item and a number on each line. From the finished drawing it makes the lists a diagram is read for (line list, equipment list, instrument index, valve list) and checks the drawing for the mistakes that are easy to miss. It is off until you switch it on: Settings › General › Add-ons › P&ID. Its tools are in the P&ID section of the right panel, as commands (below), and, when you ask for it in Settings › CadCraft › P&ID, as a group at the end of the toolbar. A drafting aid, not an engineering approval. The symbols are drawn by CadCraft after the common ideas of the standards named in \"P&ID: symbols, tags and equipment\"; the line numbers, tags and checks follow the usual habits of these diagrams, not any company's or standard's rules. A person who is responsible for the plant still decides what the diagram has to say. Your first diagram Type PIDSYMBOL, type S to choose a symbol (first its kind, then the symbol), and click where it goes. Equipment asks for its tag; the next free one is offered (P-101, T-101). Type PIDLINE, type V, Z and P for the service, size and spec (PW, 2\", A1A), and click the corners of a process line. Start and finish on a nozzle: the click lands on it. Put a valve on the line: PIDSYMBOL, a valve, and click on the line. The line is cut either side of the valve. Place an instrument, type its letters (FIC) and it takes the next loop number (FIC-101). Draw a signal line to it: PIDLINE, Y for a signal style. Type PIDCHECK and go to each finding. Type PIDLIST and click where a list goes on the sheet. Files open anywhere A symbol, a line and a list on the sheet are each saved as a block reference with its drawing inside, and its data kept with it under the application name CC_PID. A diagram saved as DWG or DXF opens in any CAD program as drawn, and in CadCraft without the add-on with nothing lost. Connections are never stored: CadCraft works them out from where things touch, so moving a pump with MOVE keeps the diagram true. Units Symbols are 4 in (100 mm in a metric drawing) by default; change it in Settings › CadCraft › P&ID. Sizes in line numbers are text: type what your drawings say (2\", DN50)."},{"slug":"pid-symbols","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: symbols, tags and equipment","summary":"The symbol library and the standards it follows, placing symbols, valves with actuators, in-line items that cut a line, and tags.","section":"tasks","order":10,"keywords":["symbol","valve","gate","globe","ball","check","butterfly","actuator","pump","vessel","tank","exchanger","instrument","balloon","tag","ISA","ISO","nozzle","connection"],"headings":[{"id":"the-library","text":"The library","level":2},{"id":"placing-symbols","text":"Placing symbols","level":2},{"id":"tags","text":"Tags","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-library\"\u003EThe library\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E places any of 52 symbols in five kinds, and a valve can carry any of five actuators.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ESymbols\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EStandard idea it follows\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EValves\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Egate, globe, ball, check, butterfly, plug, needle, diaphragm, angle, three-way, pressure relief, rupture disc\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 10628 (symbols for chemical and process engineering diagrams)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EActuators\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ediaphragm, motor, solenoid, piston, hand operated\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 10628\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Evertical and horizontal vessel, storage tank, sphere, column, reactor, shell and tube and plate exchanger, air cooler, fired heater, centrifugal and positive displacement pump, compressor, fan, filter, agitator\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 10628\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInstruments\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eballoons: field, main panel, local panel, shared display, computer function, programmable logic controller\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISA-5.1 (instrument identification: the balloon and where it is mounted)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EIn-line items\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ereducer, cap, flange, blind flange, spectacle blind, union, orifice plate, strainer, sight glass, expansion joint, flame arrestor, specification break\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 10628; orifice plate ISA-5.1; specification break ISO 14617\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EConnectors and line ends\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eoff-page connector (to, from), flow arrow, vent, drain, tie-in point\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EISO 14617 (symbols for diagrams), ISO 10628\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEvery shape is drawn by CadCraft and dedicated to the public domain (CC0 1.0): use them anywhere. The standards give the idea of a shape (a bow tie for a valve, a circle for a pump, a circle for an instrument); no vendor&#039;s symbol library and no standard&#039;s own artwork was copied or traced. The tables of letters and shapes in a standard are the standard&#039;s: for a drawing that has to follow one, check it against the standard itself.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"placing-symbols\"\u003EPlacing symbols\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDSYMBOL\u003C/code\u003E. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ES\u003C/span\u003E chooses the symbol (its kind first), \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E turns the next symbols, and for a valve \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ET\u003C/span\u003E chooses its actuator. Click to place; Enter ends.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENozzles.\u003C/strong\u003E Equipment, valves and instruments have connection points (a pump&#039;s suction and discharge, a vessel&#039;s top, bottom and sides). A click near a line end or another symbol&#039;s connection puts the new symbol&#039;s nearest connection exactly there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EIn-line items cut the line.\u003C/strong\u003E A valve, reducer, strainer or other in-line symbol clicked on a process line is turned along the line and cuts it: two pieces, each ending on the symbol. They keep the line number; one piece writes it. \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EUNDO\u003C/code\u003E puts the line back whole.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EValves with actuators\u003C/strong\u003E have one more connection, on top of the actuator, where a signal line ends. A check valve, relief valve and rupture disc take no actuator.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tags\"\u003ETags\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EEquipment, instruments and pressure relief devices need a tag; the checks say so when one is missing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEquipment\u003C/strong\u003E is offered the next free tag with a prefix by kind (\u003Ccode\u003EP-101\u003C/code\u003E pumps, \u003Ccode\u003ET-101\u003C/code\u003E tanks and spheres, \u003Ccode\u003EV-101\u003C/code\u003E vessels, \u003Ccode\u003EE-101\u003C/code\u003E exchangers, \u003Ccode\u003EC-101\u003C/code\u003E columns, \u003Ccode\u003EK-101\u003C/code\u003E compressors). Type another to use it.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EInstruments\u003C/strong\u003E ask for letters (\u003Ccode\u003EFIC\u003C/code\u003E) and take the next loop number (\u003Ccode\u003EFIC-101\u003C/code\u003E), or you type the whole tag. The first letter says what is measured, the letters after it what the instrument does with it: \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLIST\u003C/code\u003E reads them out in words (\u003Ccode\u003EFlow: indicate, control\u003C/code\u003E). A transmitter and its controller share a loop number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHand valves and in-line items\u003C/strong\u003E are not asked; give them a tag afterwards with \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDTAG\u003C/code\u003E: click the symbol, type its tag and a description. \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDTAG\u003C/code\u003E tags every untagged piece of equipment at once, numbered from left to right.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOther facts about an item (service, design pressure and temperature, capacity, material, notes) go in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, and come out in the equipment list.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The library PIDSYMBOL places any of 52 symbols in five kinds, and a valve can carry any of five actuators. Kind Symbols Standard idea it follows Valves gate, globe, ball, check, butterfly, plug, needle, diaphragm, angle, three-way, pressure relief, rupture disc ISO 10628 (symbols for chemical and process engineering diagrams) Actuators diaphragm, motor, solenoid, piston, hand operated ISO 10628 Equipment vertical and horizontal vessel, storage tank, sphere, column, reactor, shell and tube and plate exchanger, air cooler, fired heater, centrifugal and positive displacement pump, compressor, fan, filter, agitator ISO 10628 Instruments balloons: field, main panel, local panel, shared display, computer function, programmable logic controller ISA-5.1 (instrument identification: the balloon and where it is mounted) In-line items reducer, cap, flange, blind flange, spectacle blind, union, orifice plate, strainer, sight glass, expansion joint, flame arrestor, specification break ISO 10628; orifice plate ISA-5.1; specification break ISO 14617 Connectors and line ends off-page connector (to, from), flow arrow, vent, drain, tie-in point ISO 14617 (symbols for diagrams), ISO 10628 Every shape is drawn by CadCraft and dedicated to the public domain (CC0 1.0): use them anywhere. The standards give the idea of a shape (a bow tie for a valve, a circle for a pump, a circle for an instrument); no vendor's symbol library and no standard's own artwork was copied or traced. The tables of letters and shapes in a standard are the standard's: for a drawing that has to follow one, check it against the standard itself. Placing symbols Type PIDSYMBOL. S chooses the symbol (its kind first), A turns the next symbols, and for a valve T chooses its actuator. Click to place; Enter ends. Nozzles. Equipment, valves and instruments have connection points (a pump's suction and discharge, a vessel's top, bottom and sides). A click near a line end or another symbol's connection puts the new symbol's nearest connection exactly there. In-line items cut the line. A valve, reducer, strainer or other in-line symbol clicked on a process line is turned along the line and cuts it: two pieces, each ending on the symbol. They keep the line number; one piece writes it. UNDO puts the line back whole. Valves with actuators have one more connection, on top of the actuator, where a signal line ends. A check valve, relief valve and rupture disc take no actuator. Tags Equipment, instruments and pressure relief devices need a tag; the checks say so when one is missing. Equipment is offered the next free tag with a prefix by kind (P-101 pumps, T-101 tanks and spheres, V-101 vessels, E-101 exchangers, C-101 columns, K-101 compressors). Type another to use it. Instruments ask for letters (FIC) and take the next loop number (FIC-101), or you type the whole tag. The first letter says what is measured, the letters after it what the instrument does with it: PIDLIST reads them out in words (Flow: indicate, control). A transmitter and its controller share a loop number. Hand valves and in-line items are not asked; give them a tag afterwards with PIDTAG: click the symbol, type its tag and a description. A in PIDTAG tags every untagged piece of equipment at once, numbered from left to right. Other facts about an item (service, design pressure and temperature, capacity, material, notes) go in Properties, and come out in the equipment list."},{"slug":"pid-lines","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: lines and line numbers","summary":"Process lines and the four kinds of signal line, how a line number is built, and keeping one number across valves and branches.","section":"tasks","order":20,"keywords":["line","process line","line number","signal","pneumatic","electric","software","capillary","service","spec","size","sequence","arrow","branch"],"headings":[{"id":"drawing-a-line","text":"Drawing a line","level":2},{"id":"the-line-number","text":"The line number","level":2},{"id":"valves-pieces-and-branches","text":"Valves, pieces and branches","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"drawing-a-line\"\u003EDrawing a line\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLINE\u003C/code\u003E and click the corners; Enter ends. Before the first point, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EY\u003C/span\u003E chooses the kind of line, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EV\u003C/span\u003E, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EZ\u003C/span\u003E and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP\u003C/span\u003E the service, size and spec of a process line, \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EA\u003C/span\u003E the flow arrow, and \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EM\u003C/span\u003E copies the fields of a line you click. A line always starts as a process line; the service, size, spec and arrow are remembered for the next one.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA click near a nozzle, a line end or another line lands on it exactly, so what you draw is connected. A line started on the end of another process line carries on with its number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EKind\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELooks like\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProcess line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eheavy continuous line\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epipe: carries a line number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPneumatic signal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ethin line with slashes across it\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eair signal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EElectric signal\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edashed\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eelectric signal\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESoftware or data link\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edotted\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elink between control functions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECapillary tube\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edash and dot\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efilled sensing line\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe signal kinds follow the ideas of ISA-5.1; the drawing is CadCraft&#039;s own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-line-number\"\u003EThe line number\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA process line&#039;s number is built from its fields by a scheme: size, service, sequence and spec joined by dashes (\u003Ccode\u003E2&quot;-PW-101-A1A\u003C/code\u003E). A line given a service gets the next free sequence number in that service, from 101. Change the scheme in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP&amp;ID\u003C/span\u003E: put the parts in braces, in any order (\u003Ccode\u003E{svc}{seq}/{size}\u003C/code\u003E makes \u003Ccode\u003EPW101/2&quot;\u003C/code\u003E). Or type the number yourself in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E, in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELine number (typed)\u003C/span\u003E: it is used as typed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLN\u003C/code\u003E sets the service, size and spec of the lines you click; \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EN\u003C/span\u003E gives each a new number.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"valves-pieces-and-branches\"\u003EValves, pieces and branches\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA line cut by valves is several pieces with one number. The longest piece writes it (\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EWrite the number on this piece\u003C/span\u003E in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EProperties\u003C/span\u003E moves it). The line list counts the pieces as one line, from the first end to the last.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA line that ends on the middle of another line is a branch: the main&#039;s row in the line list names it. A line whose number changes where nothing sits between the two is flagged by \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDCHECK\u003C/code\u003E: put a reducer or a specification break there. A number may change at a valve.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Drawing a line Type PIDLINE and click the corners; Enter ends. Before the first point, Y chooses the kind of line, V, Z and P the service, size and spec of a process line, A the flow arrow, and M copies the fields of a line you click. A line always starts as a process line; the service, size, spec and arrow are remembered for the next one. A click near a nozzle, a line end or another line lands on it exactly, so what you draw is connected. A line started on the end of another process line carries on with its number. Kind Looks like Means Process line heavy continuous line pipe: carries a line number Pneumatic signal thin line with slashes across it air signal Electric signal dashed electric signal Software or data link dotted link between control functions Capillary tube dash and dot filled sensing line The signal kinds follow the ideas of ISA-5.1; the drawing is CadCraft's own. The line number A process line's number is built from its fields by a scheme: size, service, sequence and spec joined by dashes (2\"-PW-101-A1A). A line given a service gets the next free sequence number in that service, from 101. Change the scheme in Settings › CadCraft › P&ID: put the parts in braces, in any order ({svc}{seq}/{size} makes PW101/2\"). Or type the number yourself in Properties, in Line number (typed): it is used as typed. PIDLN sets the service, size and spec of the lines you click; N gives each a new number. Valves, pieces and branches A line cut by valves is several pieces with one number. The longest piece writes it (Write the number on this piece in Properties moves it). The line list counts the pieces as one line, from the first end to the last. A line that ends on the middle of another line is a branch: the main's row in the line list names it. A line whose number changes where nothing sits between the two is flagged by PIDCHECK: put a reducer or a specification break there. A number may change at a valve."},{"slug":"pid-lists","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: lists on the sheet, CSV and XLSX","summary":"The line list, equipment list, instrument index and valve list: on the sheet as tables that stay current, and out as one workbook or a CSV.","section":"tasks","order":30,"keywords":["list","line list","equipment list","instrument index","valve list","table","export","csv","xlsx","spreadsheet"],"headings":[{"id":"the-four-lists","text":"The four lists","level":2},{"id":"on-the-sheet","text":"On the sheet","level":2},{"id":"out-as-a-file","text":"Out as a file","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"the-four-lists\"\u003EThe four lists\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EList\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EOne row per\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EColumns\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ELine list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eline number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Esize, service, spec, insulation, from, to, branches\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epiece of equipment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etag, type, description, service, design pressure, design temperature, capacity, material, nozzles used, connected lines, notes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInstrument index\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einstrument\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etag, function in words, loop, mounting, service, description, what it is connected to\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EValve list\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Evalve and tagged in-line item\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etag, type, actuator, line, service, description, notes\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EFrom and To follow the line through valves and in-line items to the equipment, instrument or connector at each end (\u003Ccode\u003ET-101 (right)\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003EP-101 (suction)\u003C/code\u003E); an end that joins nothing says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"on-the-sheet\"\u003EOn the sheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLIST\u003C/code\u003E, choose a list and click the table&#039;s top-left corner. The table is an object: it follows the drawing, so tagging a pump, drawing a line or erasing a valve changes its rows in the same undo step. Text height is in \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESettings\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECadCraft\u003C/span\u003E › \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EP&amp;ID\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"out-as-a-file\"\u003EOut as a file\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E and choose \u003Cstrong\u003EP&amp;ID lists (XLSX workbook)\u003C/strong\u003E: one worksheet per list, a bold header row, text throughout. Or \u003Cstrong\u003EP&amp;ID lists (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E: every list in one file, each under its name. A cell that starts with \u003Ccode\u003E=\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E+\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003E-\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E@\u003C/code\u003E is written as text, never as a formula.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"The four lists List One row per Columns Line list line number size, service, spec, insulation, from, to, branches Equipment list piece of equipment tag, type, description, service, design pressure, design temperature, capacity, material, nozzles used, connected lines, notes Instrument index instrument tag, function in words, loop, mounting, service, description, what it is connected to Valve list valve and tagged in-line item tag, type, actuator, line, service, description, notes From and To follow the line through valves and in-line items to the equipment, instrument or connector at each end (T-101 (right), P-101 (suction)); an end that joins nothing says so. On the sheet Type PIDLIST, choose a list and click the table's top-left corner. The table is an object: it follows the drawing, so tagging a pump, drawing a line or erasing a valve changes its rows in the same undo step. Text height is in Settings › CadCraft › P&ID. Out as a file Type EXPORT and choose P&ID lists (XLSX workbook): one worksheet per list, a bold header row, text throughout. Or P&ID lists (CSV): every list in one file, each under its name. A cell that starts with =, +, - or @ is written as text, never as a formula."},{"slug":"pid-checks","scope":"addon:pid","title":"Check a P&ID","summary":"Find untagged items, duplicate tags, open line ends and more, and go to each one.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["pidcheck","check","error","warning","untagged","duplicate tag","open end","line number","loop","instrument"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDCHECK\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPIDC\u003C/code\u003E). The history lists every finding, errors first. Type a finding&#039;s number to zoom to it and select what it names; press \u003Ckbd class=\"hk\"\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-mac\" title=\"Return\"\u003EReturn\u003C/span\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"k-pc\"\u003EEnter\u003C/span\u003E\u003C/kbd\u003E to end.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFinding\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ELevel\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EMeans\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo tag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eequipment, an instrument or a relief device has no tag\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDuplicate tag\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo items have one tag (case does not count; off-page connectors may repeat)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen end\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eerror\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea line end joins nothing\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EOpen port\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea valve or in-line item is joined on one side only\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo line number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ea process line has no number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENo loop number\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ean instrument tag has letters but no number\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ENumber changes\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Etwo lines of different numbers meet end to end with nothing between them\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EInstrument alone\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewarning\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ean instrument has no line attached\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe checks are a drafting aid: a clean result means the drawing is consistent with itself, not that the plant is right.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Type PIDCHECK (PIDC). The history lists every finding, errors first. Type a finding's number to zoom to it and select what it names; press Enter to end. Finding Level Means No tag error equipment, an instrument or a relief device has no tag Duplicate tag error two items have one tag (case does not count; off-page connectors may repeat) Open end error a line end joins nothing Open port warning a valve or in-line item is joined on one side only No line number warning a process line has no number No loop number warning an instrument tag has letters but no number Number changes warning two lines of different numbers meet end to end with nothing between them Instrument alone warning an instrument has no line attached The checks are a drafting aid: a clean result means the drawing is consistent with itself, not that the plant is right."},{"slug":"pid-process","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: process model, stream table and control lists","summary":"Link the drawing to a process model, see its streams and line velocities on the sheet, change a line size and carry it through, and write the instrument index, I/O list and loops for the control system.","section":"tasks","order":40,"keywords":["process","simulation","flowsheet","stream table","hydraulics","velocity","pressure drop","control system","i/o list","instrument index","loop","tag list","csv"],"headings":[{"id":"the-process-model","text":"The process model","level":2},{"id":"control-system-lists","text":"Control system lists","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"the-process-model\"\u003EThe process model\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA process model is a flowsheet (units, streams, the property package) plus the links that tie it to this drawing: each unit to the equipment with the same tag, each stream to the process line that runs between the same two pieces of equipment. Open one with \u003Cstrong\u003EIMPORT\u003C/strong\u003E (a \u003Ccode\u003Eprocess.json\u003C/code\u003E made by the assistant, SuperCad Studio or Plant in BimCraft). The model is kept in the drawing, so it travels with the file.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EType \u003Cstrong\u003EPIDPROC\u003C/strong\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELink and run\u003C/strong\u003E ties what it can, solves the flowsheet and counts errors and warnings. Anything where the drawing and the flowsheet disagree (a line that enters other equipment, a size that differs between the P&amp;ID and the 3D pipe, equipment missing on either side, a velocity over the limit) is listed and shown in the Problems panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStream table\u003C/strong\u003E draws a table you can place on the sheet: each stream with its line, from and to, temperature, pressure, vapour fraction, flows, line velocity and drop, and the main mole fractions. It stays current as the model or the drawing changes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EChange a line size\u003C/strong\u003E asks for a line number and a new size, reruns the hydraulics, and writes the size on every piece of that line. The line number follows the size, and the link follows the number.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EControl lists\u003C/strong\u003E counts the tags, the I/O channels and the loops.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EPressure drop uses the line length, fittings and elevation from the 3D pipe when Plant has one, otherwise what the model says. Viscosity is an assumed default until you give the line one; the Problems panel says so.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"control-system-lists\"\u003EControl system lists\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EEXPORT\u003C/strong\u003E offers \u003Cstrong\u003EControl system lists (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003E(workbook)\u003C/strong\u003E: the tag list, the instrument index, the I/O list and the loop list, made from the tags on the drawing.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ELoops come from the instrument letters and the loop number: FT-101, FIC-101 and FV-101 are loop F101.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA transmitter is an analog input, a control valve with a positioner an analog output, a switch a digital input, a solenoid or on/off valve a digital output, a motor valve outputs and reads back its position, a pump gets start/stop and running. A controller is in the control system and has no channel of its own.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETo use other channels for one symbol, set the property \u003Ccode\u003Eio\u003C/code\u003E on it (for example \u003Ccode\u003EAI,DI\u003C/code\u003E).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe I/O list ends with a count by type and a spare allowance (20 percent unless you change it).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EChecks: duplicate tags, a valve with no controller or switch in its loop, a controller with nothing measuring.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"A drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation. The process model A process model is a flowsheet (units, streams, the property package) plus the links that tie it to this drawing: each unit to the equipment with the same tag, each stream to the process line that runs between the same two pieces of equipment. Open one with IMPORT (a process.json made by the assistant, SuperCad Studio or Plant in BimCraft). The model is kept in the drawing, so it travels with the file. Type PIDPROC: Link and run ties what it can, solves the flowsheet and counts errors and warnings. Anything where the drawing and the flowsheet disagree (a line that enters other equipment, a size that differs between the P&ID and the 3D pipe, equipment missing on either side, a velocity over the limit) is listed and shown in the Problems panel. Stream table draws a table you can place on the sheet: each stream with its line, from and to, temperature, pressure, vapour fraction, flows, line velocity and drop, and the main mole fractions. It stays current as the model or the drawing changes. Change a line size asks for a line number and a new size, reruns the hydraulics, and writes the size on every piece of that line. The line number follows the size, and the link follows the number. Control lists counts the tags, the I/O channels and the loops. Pressure drop uses the line length, fittings and elevation from the 3D pipe when Plant has one, otherwise what the model says. Viscosity is an assumed default until you give the line one; the Problems panel says so. Control system lists EXPORT offers Control system lists (CSV) and (workbook): the tag list, the instrument index, the I/O list and the loop list, made from the tags on the drawing. Loops come from the instrument letters and the loop number: FT-101, FIC-101 and FV-101 are loop F101. A transmitter is an analog input, a control valve with a positioner an analog output, a switch a digital input, a solenoid or on/off valve a digital output, a motor valve outputs and reads back its position, a pump gets start/stop and running. A controller is in the control system and has no channel of its own. To use other channels for one symbol, set the property io on it (for example AI,DI). The I/O list ends with a count by type and a spare allowance (20 percent unless you change it). Checks: duplicate tags, a valve with no controller or switch in its loop, a controller with nothing measuring."},{"slug":"pid-calc","scope":"addon:pid","title":"Process calculators","summary":"Run the process engine's calculators from the P&ID add-on: thermo, flowsheets, equipment, safety, hazardous areas, chemical stock lists, air and water, emissions, pinch, cost, structures, molecular dynamics, lab and plant data.","section":"tasks","order":45,"keywords":["pidcalc","calculator","flash","flowsheet","distillation","reactor","dynamic","batch","fermentation","heat exchanger","pressure vessel","network","water hammer","pipe stress","relief valve","mixing","hazop","lopa","sil","consequence","column","absorber","mechanism","bell-delaware","external pressure","pump trip","depressuring","blast","heavy gas","data pack","activated sludge","lims"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA calculation aid, not an engineering approval. Constants were typed without the standards open; the engines mark them for review, and an engineer has to compare them before any number is used for design. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen the \u003Cstrong\u003ECalculators\u003C/strong\u003E section of the add-on panel, or type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDCALC\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003EPCALC\u003C/code\u003E) and a calculator&#039;s name.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPick a calculator. Each one shows a sample input in plain JSON: change the numbers, the components or the list of items.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ERun\u003C/strong\u003E. The results appear as a table; a drawing (tube sheet, batch chart) or a text sheet appears under it when the calculator makes one.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EAnything the engine wants you to know (a velocity over the limit, an exchanger that is too small, a scenario above its target, a typed value not confirmed) is listed under the results and sent to the Problems panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EResults (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E saves the table. \u003Cstrong\u003EReset input\u003C/strong\u003E brings back the sample.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003ECalculator\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat it does\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eflash, vlle, purefluid\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPressure-temperature flash, bubble and dew points for a mixture; the three-phase flash (vapour and two liquids, activity models); pure-fluid properties from CoolProp, loaded the first time you ask\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eflowsheet, column, reactor\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESteady-state units with recycles, shortcut column, conversion reactor\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Edynamics, tune\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ETanks, valves and controllers over time with a trend chart; controller tuning from a step test\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ebatch, batchbal, ferment\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA batch recipe schedule with equipment occupancy; a batch mass balance; Monod fermentation\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eengine\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ESolve with our engine or a permissively licensed add-on; GPL programs are refused\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eexchanger, vessel\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EShell-and-tube rating and tube sheet; ASME VIII shell, head, nozzle and data sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Enetwork, hammer, pipestress, relief\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPipe network with pumps; water hammer; pipe stress with bend flexibility, tee factors, lifting rests and friction; relief sizing for gas, steam, liquid and fire, with a rupture disc, the outlet line and the thrust\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Emixing\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMixing time on the finite-volume flow solver, or on the lattice Boltzmann method (a stirred tank with an impeller stand-in, or the residence time distribution of a channel); laminar and two-dimensional, no turbulence model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ehazop, lopa, consequence\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHAZOP worksheet, LOPA (also from a HAZOP row) and SIF verification, release and fire or blast zones\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Earea\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EHazardous area classification: release sources to Zone 0, 1 or 2 with an extent from the dilution volume; the pack is a simplified screening rule, not the standard&#039;s table\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Einventory\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EWhat is stored where, the 16 safety data sheet sections, a starter list of dangerous pairs in one room, and threshold checks against a pack (the bundled one is made up)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eair\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStack screening: plume rise, the worst case concentration and where it lands, from a Gaussian plume; a screening aid, not a permit model\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewater\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EActivated sludge plant (the published ASM1 model, steady state, COD and nitrogen balance), screens, clarifier and disinfection\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eemissions\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECarbon dioxide equivalent of vented and burned streams and fired duty, with the factor sources named; no electricity factor is assumed\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epinch\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EProblem table, composite curves, energy targets, the minimum number of units and the temperature difference trade-off\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecost\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EEquipment cost correlations, installation factors and operating cost; the cost index comes from a data pack or a pasted year and value list, the energy price is yours, nothing is assumed; the equipment list can leave as a TakeoffCraft pack\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Estructure, md\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA structure from SMILES or drawn with the mouse, with a drawing, formula and group-contribution estimates (and RDKit descriptors when you tick it); a small molecular dynamics run\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecolumn2, mechanism\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA faster inside-out column solver and an absorber or stripper (sum-rates); a reaction mechanism file in YAML run in a closed vessel (reactions the loader cannot read are listed, never guessed)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ebell, vessel2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EBell-Delaware geometry and, with your own coefficient pack, the shell-side rating; external pressure and stiffening ring from the chart pairs you type, the vessel body outline and a shell nozzle\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Efittingk\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EFitting losses: K and head loss of a fitting or valve from the 2-K (Hooper) and 3-K (Darby) tables, picked from a list. The bundled table is a draft\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ecompressor, npsh\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003ECompressor head, shaft work, power and outlet temperature for an ideal gas (polytropic or isentropic efficiency, optional surge-line margin); pump head from its curve and the NPSH margin against the maker&#039;s required points\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epumptrip, pipestress2, relief2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EPump trip with check valve and run-down; pressure thrust, spring hanger choice from a maker&#039;s table and stop gaps; depressuring, control-valve failure and the load of each relief header\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eblast, heavygas, packs\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EMulti-energy blast from your own curve pack; a heavy-gas box model with your own constants; thresholds, reactive pairs and area classification built from a table (every row needs its clause)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Epme\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA check of the long-range electrostatics method: smooth particle-mesh Ewald against the plain sum on random charges, and rigid bonds held by SHAKE\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ewdesign, lims2\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EActivated sludge tank, return ratio and clarifier by the design equations; the settings check for your own lab system (the secret&#039;s name is all that is kept)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Elims, historian\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EA lab result from your own system or a CSV becomes a proposed stream you accept; plant tags read from a CSV become values with their limits (OPC UA needs CadCraft Studio)\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHazard zones on the sheet.\u003C/strong\u003E After the area calculator, type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDZONE\u003C/code\u003E and choose plan or section: circles and chords are drawn on the layers AREA-ZONE0, AREA-ZONE1 and AREA-ZONE2, in one undo step. Take the extent from the standard before relying on it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHAZOP copilot.\u003C/strong\u003E When you connect your own assistant, its tools can read the nodes proposed from this P&amp;ID and suggest HAZOP rows. Rows are saved with the drawing as suggestions only. Type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDHAZOP\u003C/code\u003E (or the Review HAZOP button): it lists them and a named person accepts or rejects each one. The assistant has no tool to do that.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ENot built yet: a form for each input (the box takes JSON), saving a calculation with the drawing, release sources read from the P&amp;ID, plumes on the site plan, and a heat-exchanger network grid.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ENot engineer-reviewed.\u003C/strong\u003E The vessel, pipe stress, relief, consequence and area calculators say so on every result and in the Problems; one engineer will review the five together before any number is used for design.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESite plan and 3D.\u003C/strong\u003E After the consequence calculator the \u003Cstrong\u003ESite plan (GeoJSON)\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003ESite plan (DXF)\u003C/strong\u003E buttons save the zones in metres for CivilCraft&#039;s Import; \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDCONSEQ\u003C/code\u003E draws them on the sheet (layers CONSEQ-1, CONSEQ-2 and so on). In BimCraft&#039;s Plant panel the same zones become solids in the model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESources from the drawing.\u003C/strong\u003E With the area calculator, \u003Cstrong\u003ESources from the drawing\u003C/strong\u003E reads pumps, vessels, valves and other symbols of the drawing as release sources, grade by what each is (seals and stems secondary, vents primary); \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDSOURCES\u003C/code\u003E does the same from the command line and asks which substance each service holds. The assumptions are listed in the Problems.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFlare header from the relief loads.\u003C/strong\u003E In the relief extras calculator (\u003Ccode\u003Erelief2\u003C/code\u003E), give \u003Ccode\u003Eheader.flare\u003C/code\u003E a gas network whose valve nodes carry the same names as the devices in your scenarios. The loads of the governing case of that header become the demands of the network, and the calculator reports the back pressure at each valve as a share of its set pressure. A valve over its limit is listed in Problems. The network and the set pressures are yours; nothing is assumed.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECharges and rigid bonds in the molecular run.\u003C/strong\u003E The molecular dynamics calculator (\u003Ccode\u003Emd\u003C/code\u003E) takes \u003Ccode\u003Eq\u003C/code\u003E (charge of plus and minus q on alternate atoms), \u003Ccode\u003Epme\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E{ grid, order }\u003C/code\u003E) to use the smooth particle-mesh Ewald sum instead of the plain one, and \u003Ccode\u003Econstraints\u003C/code\u003E (\u003Ccode\u003E[[i, j, d]]\u003C/code\u003E) to hold bonds rigid. The run says which long-range sum it used.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETest the connection to your own lab system.\u003C/strong\u003E In the lab-system calculator (\u003Ccode\u003Elims2\u003C/code\u003E) set \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;test&quot;: true\u003C/code\u003E. Inside CadCraft Studio the connection is walked step by step (settings, reach the system, list samples, read one, read its results) and the failing step is named; Studio adds your secret and pins the address. In a browser tab nothing is sent and the calculator says it needs Studio.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFitting losses (draft table).\u003C/strong\u003E The \u003Ccode\u003Efittingk\u003C/code\u003E calculator lists 32 fittings of the 2-K method and 34 of the 3-K method in the list above its input; pick one, give the Reynolds number and the pipe size in inches (the actual inside diameter for 2-K, the nominal size for 3-K) and, if you like, the velocity, and it returns K and the head loss. \u003Cstrong\u003EThe table is a draft.\u003C/strong\u003E Its numbers were taken from the tables of the MIT-licensed \u003Ccode\u003Efluids\u003C/code\u003E library for the two methods (Hooper, Chem. Eng. 88(17), 1981; Darby, Chem. Eng. 106(7), 1999) and have not been checked against the papers by an engineer; six entries carry a note where the source&#039;s name looks odd. Every result says so. Set \u003Ccode\u003E&quot;pack&quot;: null\u003C/code\u003E for no table (it then says what is needed) or give your own \u003Ccode\u003Ecadcraft-fitting-k-pack\u003C/code\u003E. A pipe network takes one K per link: put the K you got on the link. The network solver does not look the K up itself as the flow changes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EFittings in a pipe network.\u003C/strong\u003E In the pipe network calculator (\u003Ccode\u003Enetwork\u003C/code\u003E) a link may carry \u003Ccode\u003Efit\u003C/code\u003E: a list of fittings from the fitting table (\u003Ccode\u003E&quot;fittingPack&quot;: &quot;draft&quot;\u003C/code\u003E is the bundled draft table), each with an optional count. At every step of the solution the K of the list is worked out from that link&#039;s own Reynolds number and diameter and added to the K you typed, so a slower flow gets the larger K. The result gives each link&#039;s K, the fittings&#039; share and Re. Without a table the lists are ignored, with a warning, and the typed K stands. The draft label above applies. A link of kind \u003Ccode\u003Ecvalve\u003C/code\u003E is a control valve given by its Kv and opening (liquid lines; a control valve on a gas line is not built, give it as a pipe with K).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ECompressor and pump.\u003C/strong\u003E The compressor calculator uses the textbook ideal-gas relations with a constant k and a constant Z that you give, adiabatic and without inter-cooling; it states these in the results. Give the efficiency as polytropic or isentropic and it returns both. A real gas near its critical point, or a machine with inter-coolers, needs an equation of state. The pump calculator reads the head from the pump points you give and subtracts NPSH required (the maker&#039;s points) from NPSH available; the margin you need is your project&#039;s decision.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERelief valves.\u003C/strong\u003E \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDRELIEF\u003C/code\u003E sizes the relief valve of a tag with the relief calculator&#039;s inputs and writes the orifice letter and the required area on the symbol.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDrawing a structure.\u003C/strong\u003E Pick the structure calculator and a small editor opens: click to place atoms, click two atoms to join them (again for a double or triple bond), the ring buttons place a ring, \u003Cstrong\u003EUse this structure\u003C/strong\u003E puts its SMILES into the input.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A calculation aid, not an engineering approval. Constants were typed without the standards open; the engines mark them for review, and an engineer has to compare them before any number is used for design. No representation. Open the Calculators section of the add-on panel, or type PIDCALC (PCALC) and a calculator's name. Pick a calculator. Each one shows a sample input in plain JSON: change the numbers, the components or the list of items. Press Run. The results appear as a table; a drawing (tube sheet, batch chart) or a text sheet appears under it when the calculator makes one. Anything the engine wants you to know (a velocity over the limit, an exchanger that is too small, a scenario above its target, a typed value not confirmed) is listed under the results and sent to the Problems panel. Results (CSV) saves the table. Reset input brings back the sample. Calculator What it does flash, vlle, purefluid Pressure-temperature flash, bubble and dew points for a mixture; the three-phase flash (vapour and two liquids, activity models); pure-fluid properties from CoolProp, loaded the first time you ask flowsheet, column, reactor Steady-state units with recycles, shortcut column, conversion reactor dynamics, tune Tanks, valves and controllers over time with a trend chart; controller tuning from a step test batch, batchbal, ferment A batch recipe schedule with equipment occupancy; a batch mass balance; Monod fermentation engine Solve with our engine or a permissively licensed add-on; GPL programs are refused exchanger, vessel Shell-and-tube rating and tube sheet; ASME VIII shell, head, nozzle and data sheet network, hammer, pipestress, relief Pipe network with pumps; water hammer; pipe stress with bend flexibility, tee factors, lifting rests and friction; relief sizing for gas, steam, liquid and fire, with a rupture disc, the outlet line and the thrust mixing Mixing time on the finite-volume flow solver, or on the lattice Boltzmann method (a stirred tank with an impeller stand-in, or the residence time distribution of a channel); laminar and two-dimensional, no turbulence model hazop, lopa, consequence HAZOP worksheet, LOPA (also from a HAZOP row) and SIF verification, release and fire or blast zones area Hazardous area classification: release sources to Zone 0, 1 or 2 with an extent from the dilution volume; the pack is a simplified screening rule, not the standard's table inventory What is stored where, the 16 safety data sheet sections, a starter list of dangerous pairs in one room, and threshold checks against a pack (the bundled one is made up) air Stack screening: plume rise, the worst case concentration and where it lands, from a Gaussian plume; a screening aid, not a permit model water Activated sludge plant (the published ASM1 model, steady state, COD and nitrogen balance), screens, clarifier and disinfection emissions Carbon dioxide equivalent of vented and burned streams and fired duty, with the factor sources named; no electricity factor is assumed pinch Problem table, composite curves, energy targets, the minimum number of units and the temperature difference trade-off cost Equipment cost correlations, installation factors and operating cost; the cost index comes from a data pack or a pasted year and value list, the energy price is yours, nothing is assumed; the equipment list can leave as a TakeoffCraft pack structure, md A structure from SMILES or drawn with the mouse, with a drawing, formula and group-contribution estimates (and RDKit descriptors when you tick it); a small molecular dynamics run column2, mechanism A faster inside-out column solver and an absorber or stripper (sum-rates); a reaction mechanism file in YAML run in a closed vessel (reactions the loader cannot read are listed, never guessed) bell, vessel2 Bell-Delaware geometry and, with your own coefficient pack, the shell-side rating; external pressure and stiffening ring from the chart pairs you type, the vessel body outline and a shell nozzle fittingk Fitting losses: K and head loss of a fitting or valve from the 2-K (Hooper) and 3-K (Darby) tables, picked from a list. The bundled table is a draft compressor, npsh Compressor head, shaft work, power and outlet temperature for an ideal gas (polytropic or isentropic efficiency, optional surge-line margin); pump head from its curve and the NPSH margin against the maker's required points pumptrip, pipestress2, relief2 Pump trip with check valve and run-down; pressure thrust, spring hanger choice from a maker's table and stop gaps; depressuring, control-valve failure and the load of each relief header blast, heavygas, packs Multi-energy blast from your own curve pack; a heavy-gas box model with your own constants; thresholds, reactive pairs and area classification built from a table (every row needs its clause) pme A check of the long-range electrostatics method: smooth particle-mesh Ewald against the plain sum on random charges, and rigid bonds held by SHAKE wdesign, lims2 Activated sludge tank, return ratio and clarifier by the design equations; the settings check for your own lab system (the secret's name is all that is kept) lims, historian A lab result from your own system or a CSV becomes a proposed stream you accept; plant tags read from a CSV become values with their limits (OPC UA needs CadCraft Studio) Hazard zones on the sheet. After the area calculator, type PIDZONE and choose plan or section: circles and chords are drawn on the layers AREA-ZONE0, AREA-ZONE1 and AREA-ZONE2, in one undo step. Take the extent from the standard before relying on it. HAZOP copilot. When you connect your own assistant, its tools can read the nodes proposed from this P&ID and suggest HAZOP rows. Rows are saved with the drawing as suggestions only. Type PIDHAZOP (or the Review HAZOP button): it lists them and a named person accepts or rejects each one. The assistant has no tool to do that. Not built yet: a form for each input (the box takes JSON), saving a calculation with the drawing, release sources read from the P&ID, plumes on the site plan, and a heat-exchanger network grid. Not engineer-reviewed. The vessel, pipe stress, relief, consequence and area calculators say so on every result and in the Problems; one engineer will review the five together before any number is used for design. Site plan and 3D. After the consequence calculator the Site plan (GeoJSON) and Site plan (DXF) buttons save the zones in metres for CivilCraft's Import; PIDCONSEQ draws them on the sheet (layers CONSEQ-1, CONSEQ-2 and so on). In BimCraft's Plant panel the same zones become solids in the model. Sources from the drawing. With the area calculator, Sources from the drawing reads pumps, vessels, valves and other symbols of the drawing as release sources, grade by what each is (seals and stems secondary, vents primary); PIDSOURCES does the same from the command line and asks which substance each service holds. The assumptions are listed in the Problems. Flare header from the relief loads. In the relief extras calculator (relief2), give header.flare a gas network whose valve nodes carry the same names as the devices in your scenarios. The loads of the governing case of that header become the demands of the network, and the calculator reports the back pressure at each valve as a share of its set pressure. A valve over its limit is listed in Problems. The network and the set pressures are yours; nothing is assumed. Charges and rigid bonds in the molecular run. The molecular dynamics calculator (md) takes q (charge of plus and minus q on alternate atoms), pme ({ grid, order }) to use the smooth particle-mesh Ewald sum instead of the plain one, and constraints ([[i, j, d]]) to hold bonds rigid. The run says which long-range sum it used. Test the connection to your own lab system. In the lab-system calculator (lims2) set \"test\": true. Inside CadCraft Studio the connection is walked step by step (settings, reach the system, list samples, read one, read its results) and the failing step is named; Studio adds your secret and pins the address. In a browser tab nothing is sent and the calculator says it needs Studio. Fitting losses (draft table). The fittingk calculator lists 32 fittings of the 2-K method and 34 of the 3-K method in the list above its input; pick one, give the Reynolds number and the pipe size in inches (the actual inside diameter for 2-K, the nominal size for 3-K) and, if you like, the velocity, and it returns K and the head loss. The table is a draft. Its numbers were taken from the tables of the MIT-licensed fluids library for the two methods (Hooper, Chem. Eng. 88(17), 1981; Darby, Chem. Eng. 106(7), 1999) and have not been checked against the papers by an engineer; six entries carry a note where the source's name looks odd. Every result says so. Set \"pack\": null for no table (it then says what is needed) or give your own cadcraft-fitting-k-pack. A pipe network takes one K per link: put the K you got on the link. The network solver does not look the K up itself as the flow changes. Fittings in a pipe network. In the pipe network calculator (network) a link may carry fit: a list of fittings from the fitting table (\"fittingPack\": \"draft\" is the bundled draft table), each with an optional count. At every step of the solution the K of the list is worked out from that link's own Reynolds number and diameter and added to the K you typed, so a slower flow gets the larger K. The result gives each link's K, the fittings' share and Re. Without a table the lists are ignored, with a warning, and the typed K stands. The draft label above applies. A link of kind cvalve is a control valve given by its Kv and opening (liquid lines; a control valve on a gas line is not built, give it as a pipe with K). Compressor and pump. The compressor calculator uses the textbook ideal-gas relations with a constant k and a constant Z that you give, adiabatic and without inter-cooling; it states these in the results. Give the efficiency as polytropic or isentropic and it returns both. A real gas near its critical point, or a machine with inter-coolers, needs an equation of state. The pump calculator reads the head from the pump points you give and subtracts NPSH required (the maker's points) from NPSH available; the margin you need is your project's decision. Relief valves. PIDRELIEF sizes the relief valve of a tag with the relief calculator's inputs and writes the orifice letter and the required area on the symbol. Drawing a structure. Pick the structure calculator and a small editor opens: click to place atoms, click two atoms to join them (again for a double or triple bond), the ring buttons place a ring, Use this structure puts its SMILES into the input."},{"slug":"pid-draw","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: flow diagram, heat-exchanger network, HAZOP nodes and live values","summary":"Draw the process flow diagram and the heat-exchanger network grid, mark HAZOP nodes on the sheet, put plant values beside the symbols, and draw a tube sheet or a plume.","section":"tasks","order":46,"keywords":["pfd","process flow diagram","heat exchanger network","pinch","grid","hazop node","markupcraft","live","historian","opc ua","tube sheet","plume","running totals"],"headings":[{"id":"process-flow-diagram","text":"Process flow diagram","level":2},{"id":"heat-exchanger-network","text":"Heat-exchanger network","level":2},{"id":"hazop-nodes","text":"HAZOP nodes","level":2},{"id":"live-values","text":"Live values","level":2},{"id":"tube-sheet-and-plume","text":"Tube sheet and plume","level":2},{"id":"running-totals","text":"Running totals","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"process-flow-diagram\"\u003EProcess flow diagram\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWith a process model in the drawing (IMPORT, then \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDPROC\u003C/code\u003E), type \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDPFD\u003C/code\u003E and pick a corner. The units are drawn in columns by flow, streams get arrows with their name, temperature and pressure, recycles run over the top, and the stream table goes under the diagram. One undo step.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"heat-exchanger-network\"\u003EHeat-exchanger network\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003ERun the pinch calculator first, then \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDHEN\u003C/code\u003E. It asks for the smallest temperature difference and the stream data (supply and target temperature, CP in kW/K), then draws the grid: hot streams on top, cold below, the pinch line, each match as a pair of circles with its duty, heaters and coolers. The Problems panel says how many units there are against the minimum and warns about a match that leaves less than the smallest difference. This is a first design by the pinch method; stream splitting is not done.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"hazop-nodes\"\u003EHAZOP nodes\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDNODE\u003C/code\u003E: pick two corners, give the node a name and its design intent. The equipment tags and line numbers inside the boundary go into the node, the boundary and its label are drawn on the layer HAZOP-NODE, and the node is saved with the drawing (it travels in DWG and DXF as the hidden safety block). Export gives the worksheet as \u003Cstrong\u003EXLSX\u003C/strong\u003E or \u003Cstrong\u003EPDF\u003C/strong\u003E, and the node boxes as a JSON file for MarkupCraft. A team study aid: the guide words and the risk matrix are vocabulary and an example, a facilitated team does the study.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"live-values\"\u003ELive values\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDLIVE\u003C/code\u003E puts readings beside the tagged symbols, coloured by state (ok, high, low, stale, bad), on layers LIVE-OK, LIVE-HIGH and so on; a new reading replaces the old labels in one undo step. Give the readings as CSV with the header \u003Ccode\u003Etag,time,value,quality\u003C/code\u003E, or press Enter to read Studio&#039;s plant connection (the browser alone cannot reach a plant). Read only: nothing here controls anything, and nothing here is an alarm system.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"tube-sheet-and-plume\"\u003ETube sheet and plume\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDTUBESHEET\u003C/code\u003E draws the tube sheet of the exchanger in the exchanger calculator (shell, every tube, the pass lane). \u003Ccode class=\"hcmd\"\u003EPIDPLUME\u003C/code\u003E draws the contours of the stack in the air calculator with the stack, a wind arrow and the note that this is a screening, not AERMOD and not a permit model.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"running-totals\"\u003ERunning totals\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EWhen the app shows running totals, the emissions, cost and pinch-free batch calculators feed the status bar: process emissions per year, the installed plant cost and the batch campaign length. A figure appears only after you ran that calculator.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A drafting and study aid, not an engineering approval. No representation. Process flow diagram With a process model in the drawing (IMPORT, then PIDPROC), type PIDPFD and pick a corner. The units are drawn in columns by flow, streams get arrows with their name, temperature and pressure, recycles run over the top, and the stream table goes under the diagram. One undo step. Heat-exchanger network Run the pinch calculator first, then PIDHEN. It asks for the smallest temperature difference and the stream data (supply and target temperature, CP in kW/K), then draws the grid: hot streams on top, cold below, the pinch line, each match as a pair of circles with its duty, heaters and coolers. The Problems panel says how many units there are against the minimum and warns about a match that leaves less than the smallest difference. This is a first design by the pinch method; stream splitting is not done. HAZOP nodes PIDNODE: pick two corners, give the node a name and its design intent. The equipment tags and line numbers inside the boundary go into the node, the boundary and its label are drawn on the layer HAZOP-NODE, and the node is saved with the drawing (it travels in DWG and DXF as the hidden safety block). Export gives the worksheet as XLSX or PDF, and the node boxes as a JSON file for MarkupCraft. A team study aid: the guide words and the risk matrix are vocabulary and an example, a facilitated team does the study. Live values PIDLIVE puts readings beside the tagged symbols, coloured by state (ok, high, low, stale, bad), on layers LIVE-OK, LIVE-HIGH and so on; a new reading replaces the old labels in one undo step. Give the readings as CSV with the header tag,time,value,quality, or press Enter to read Studio's plant connection (the browser alone cannot reach a plant). Read only: nothing here controls anything, and nothing here is an alarm system. Tube sheet and plume PIDTUBESHEET draws the tube sheet of the exchanger in the exchanger calculator (shell, every tube, the pass lane). PIDPLUME draws the contours of the stack in the air calculator with the stack, a wind arrow and the note that this is a screening, not AERMOD and not a permit model. Running totals When the app shows running totals, the emissions, cost and pinch-free batch calculators feed the status bar: process emissions per year, the installed plant cost and the batch campaign length. A figure appears only after you ran that calculator."},{"slug":"pid-loops","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: loop binding and faceplates","summary":"Tie each controller of the drawing to what it reads and drives in a plant model, run the loops, and work a faceplate in auto or manual.","section":"tasks","order":47,"keywords":["loop","controller","faceplate","pid","tuning","simulation","set point","auto","manual","binding"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA model, not a plant: nothing here talks to equipment. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003ELoops and faceplates\u003C/strong\u003E in the add-on panel. Every controller of the drawing (an instrument that sits in a loop) has a row: what it reads (\u003Ccode\u003ET1.h\u003C/code\u003E for the level of tank T1), what it drives (\u003Ccode\u003Evalve.V1\u003C/code\u003E), the set point, the gain, the reset time and the action. The plant model below is JSON: tanks, reactors, valves and sources, as in the dynamic simulation calculator.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFill in the rows. A controller without a measurement and an output is listed as not tied; the simulation leaves it out and says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003ERun the loops\u003C/strong\u003E. Each controller gets a faceplate: mode, SP, PV, OUT and the error, with a trend of the measurement and the output.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETo manual\u003C/strong\u003E holds the output where it was, and you can type another; \u003Cstrong\u003ETo auto\u003C/strong\u003E hands it back. Changing the set point runs the model again.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EIf the numerical solver cannot follow a loop (a gain too high for the model), the panel says it ran out of steps; lower the gain or lengthen the reset time.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A model, not a plant: nothing here talks to equipment. No representation. Open Loops and faceplates in the add-on panel. Every controller of the drawing (an instrument that sits in a loop) has a row: what it reads (T1.h for the level of tank T1), what it drives (valve.V1), the set point, the gain, the reset time and the action. The plant model below is JSON: tanks, reactors, valves and sources, as in the dynamic simulation calculator. Fill in the rows. A controller without a measurement and an output is listed as not tied; the simulation leaves it out and says so. Press Run the loops. Each controller gets a faceplate: mode, SP, PV, OUT and the error, with a trend of the measurement and the output. To manual holds the output where it was, and you can type another; To auto hands it back. Changing the set point runs the model again. If the numerical solver cannot follow a loop (a gain too high for the model), the panel says it ran out of steps; lower the gain or lengthen the reset time."},{"slug":"pid-hazop-timer","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: HAZOP session timer","summary":"Keep a HAZOP or What-If session on time, node by node, with a clock for the node on the table, over-time shown, and the times as CSV.","section":"tasks","order":48,"keywords":["hazop","what-if","timer","facilitation","session","node","clock","minutes","csv","pid"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EA study aid for the team. It records the time; it does not decide that a node is finished. No representation.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EHAZOP session timer\u003C/strong\u003E in the add-on panel. It lists the nodes you drew with \u003Ccode\u003EPIDNODE\u003C/code\u003E, one row each, with its minutes.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EType the session total and press \u003Cstrong\u003EShare the minutes out\u003C/strong\u003E. Nodes with more guide word and parameter pairs still to cover get more minutes (at least 5 each); with nothing covered yet the minutes are even. Change any node with \u003Cstrong\u003E+5 min\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cstrong\u003EStart\u003C/strong\u003E on the node the team is discussing. The big clock counts down; past zero it shows the over-time in red and keeps counting. Nothing is stopped for you.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EPause\u003C/strong\u003E and \u003Cstrong\u003EResume\u003C/strong\u003E hold the clock, \u003Cstrong\u003EDone, next node\u003C/strong\u003E marks the node done and starts the next one that is not done, and \u003Cstrong\u003EReset all\u003C/strong\u003E clears the times.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETimes (CSV)\u003C/strong\u003E saves node, planned, used and over minutes.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe times are kept with the add-on&#039;s storage, so a closed page keeps them. A node you draw or rename later joins the list without losing the time already used.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"A study aid for the team. It records the time; it does not decide that a node is finished. No representation. Open HAZOP session timer in the add-on panel. It lists the nodes you drew with PIDNODE, one row each, with its minutes. Type the session total and press Share the minutes out. Nodes with more guide word and parameter pairs still to cover get more minutes (at least 5 each); with nothing covered yet the minutes are even. Change any node with +5 min. Press Start on the node the team is discussing. The big clock counts down; past zero it shows the over-time in red and keeps counting. Nothing is stopped for you. Pause and Resume hold the clock, Done, next node marks the node done and starts the next one that is not done, and Reset all clears the times. Times (CSV) saves node, planned, used and over minutes. The times are kept with the add-on's storage, so a closed page keeps them. A node you draw or rename later joins the list without losing the time already used."},{"slug":"pid-connect","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: connect to a device and the tag map","summary":"List the plant devices, type the tag map, try it with a CSV of readings, and see the values beside the tags on the drawing. Read only.","section":"tasks","order":49,"keywords":["live","device","modbus","mqtt","bacnet","opc ua","tag map","readings","csv","studio","historian"],"headings":[],"html":"\u003Cp\u003ERead only: nothing here writes to a device, raises an alarm or acts as a safety function.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cstrong\u003EConnect to a device\u003C/strong\u003E in the add-on panel.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EDevices.\u003C/strong\u003E Add each device: a name, the protocol (Modbus TCP, MQTT, BACnet/IP or OPC UA), the host and port. A device on a public address needs your tick on \u003Cstrong\u003EI approve this host\u003C/strong\u003E; one on a private network does not.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETag map.\u003C/strong\u003E One row per tag: id, device, address, label, unit, scale, offset, low and high limits, type and the tag of the drawing it belongs to. Press \u003Cstrong\u003ECheck the map\u003C/strong\u003E; the notes (a missing device, an address that is wrong for the protocol, limits the wrong way round) go to Problems.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ETry it with readings.\u003C/strong\u003E Paste a CSV with \u003Ccode\u003Etag,time,value,quality\u003C/code\u003E and press \u003Cstrong\u003EShow these readings\u003C/strong\u003E. The values are drawn beside the matching symbols. This works in any browser.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ERead the devices\u003C/strong\u003E needs CadCraft Studio, because a browser page cannot open a network connection to a plant. Without it the panel says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe map is kept with the add-on&#039;s storage on this computer; it never holds a password.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"Read only: nothing here writes to a device, raises an alarm or acts as a safety function. Open Connect to a device in the add-on panel. Devices. Add each device: a name, the protocol (Modbus TCP, MQTT, BACnet/IP or OPC UA), the host and port. A device on a public address needs your tick on I approve this host; one on a private network does not. Tag map. One row per tag: id, device, address, label, unit, scale, offset, low and high limits, type and the tag of the drawing it belongs to. Press Check the map; the notes (a missing device, an address that is wrong for the protocol, limits the wrong way round) go to Problems. Try it with readings. Paste a CSV with tag,time,value,quality and press Show these readings. The values are drawn beside the matching symbols. This works in any browser. Read the devices needs CadCraft Studio, because a browser page cannot open a network connection to a plant. Without it the panel says so. The map is kept with the add-on's storage on this computer; it never holds a password."},{"slug":"pid-bring-over","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: bring a job over","summary":"Read a DEXPI file or a stream table from another tool into the drawing, and write the diagram out as DEXPI, with one short guide for each type of tool the job comes from.","section":"switching","order":20,"keywords":["dexpi","proteus","xml","import","export","stream table","heat and material balance","csv","xlsx","spreadsheet","move a job","other software","interchange"],"headings":[{"id":"what-you-ll-do","text":"What you'll do","level":2},{"id":"from-a-p-id-or-plant-design-program","text":"From a P&ID or plant-design program","level":2},{"id":"from-a-process-simulator-or-a-datasheet","text":"From a process simulator or a datasheet","level":2},{"id":"check-what-came-over","text":"Check what came over","level":2},{"id":"sending-a-job-back","text":"Sending a job back","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"what-you-ll-do\"\u003EWhat you&#039;ll do\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EMove one job across without redrawing it. The add-on reads two kinds of file and writes one:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cdiv class=\"ht-wrap\"\u003E\u003Ctable class=\"ht\"\u003E\u003Cthead\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EFile\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EDirection\u003C/th\u003E\u003Cth scope=\"col\"\u003EWhat comes over\u003C/th\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/thead\u003E\u003Ctbody\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EDEXPI plant model (ProteusXML, \u003Ccode\u003E.xml\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ein and out\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Eequipment with tags and nozzles, piping network systems and their segments, valves and in-line parts, instrumentation, line data, connections, positions\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003Ctr\u003E\u003Ctd\u003EStream table (\u003Ccode\u003E.csv\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003E.xlsx\u003C/code\u003E)\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Ein\u003C/td\u003E\u003Ctd\u003Estream names, temperature, pressure, vapour fraction, flows, composition, as a table on the sheet\u003C/td\u003E\u003C/tr\u003E\u003C/tbody\u003E\u003C/table\u003E\u003C/div\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EUse the \u003Ccode\u003EIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E command and pick the file type, or \u003Ccode\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E for DEXPI. Everything the reader could not bring is listed once, by kind, in the Problems panel under &quot;Imported files&quot;.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-a-p-id-or-plant-design-program\"\u003EFrom a P&amp;ID or plant-design program\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EIn the other tool, export the P&amp;ID as DEXPI (ProteusXML), one drawing at a time.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun \u003Ccode\u003EIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E and choose &quot;DEXPI plant model&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EItems with a position in the file are placed there. Items without one are laid out in a grid below the drawing: move them where they belong.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun \u003Ccode\u003EPIDCHECK\u003C/code\u003E and open the line list: line numbers and sizes should match the source.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-note\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003ENote\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe drawing graphics in the file are not used. Symbols are drawn again from this add-on&#039;s own library, so a symbol class it has no symbol for is listed and not placed. Signal lines are not drawn; the instruments they join are.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"from-a-process-simulator-or-a-datasheet\"\u003EFrom a process simulator or a datasheet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EExport the stream (heat and material balance) table to CSV or Excel. Streams may run across or down. Keep the unit in the label, as in &quot;Temperature (°C)&quot;, or in a Units column.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERun \u003Ccode\u003EIMPORT\u003C/code\u003E and choose &quot;Stream table&quot;.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EThe table is put on the sheet. A pressure marked gauge is made absolute; a column with no unit is read as °C and bar; each is noted in the Problems panel.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA table has no topology: it does not bring the flowsheet itself, only the stream conditions.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"check-what-came-over\"\u003ECheck what came over\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECompare the equipment list with the source&#039;s equipment list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ECompare two streams you know by hand: flow, temperature and the composition sum.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EConnections that point at nothing are reported once, as a count.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"sending-a-job-back\"\u003ESending a job back\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003E\u003Ccode\u003EEXPORT\u003C/code\u003E and choose &quot;DEXPI plant model&quot;. Each line number becomes a piping network system, each drawn piece of line a segment with its centre line, equipment gets a nozzle at every connection, and the valve&#039;s actuator is written inside the valve. The class names follow the DEXPI test cases; the file is for exchange, not an engineering approval.\u003C/p\u003E","text":"What you'll do Move one job across without redrawing it. The add-on reads two kinds of file and writes one: File Direction What comes over DEXPI plant model (ProteusXML, .xml) in and out equipment with tags and nozzles, piping network systems and their segments, valves and in-line parts, instrumentation, line data, connections, positions Stream table (.csv or .xlsx) in stream names, temperature, pressure, vapour fraction, flows, composition, as a table on the sheet Use the IMPORT command and pick the file type, or EXPORT for DEXPI. Everything the reader could not bring is listed once, by kind, in the Problems panel under \"Imported files\". From a P&ID or plant-design program In the other tool, export the P&ID as DEXPI (ProteusXML), one drawing at a time. Run IMPORT and choose \"DEXPI plant model\". Items with a position in the file are placed there. Items without one are laid out in a grid below the drawing: move them where they belong. Run PIDCHECK and open the line list: line numbers and sizes should match the source. The drawing graphics in the file are not used. Symbols are drawn again from this add-on's own library, so a symbol class it has no symbol for is listed and not placed. Signal lines are not drawn; the instruments they join are. From a process simulator or a datasheet Export the stream (heat and material balance) table to CSV or Excel. Streams may run across or down. Keep the unit in the label, as in \"Temperature (°C)\", or in a Units column. Run IMPORT and choose \"Stream table\". The table is put on the sheet. A pressure marked gauge is made absolute; a column with no unit is read as °C and bar; each is noted in the Problems panel. A table has no topology: it does not bring the flowsheet itself, only the stream conditions. Check what came over Compare the equipment list with the source's equipment list. Compare two streams you know by hand: flow, temperature and the composition sum. Connections that point at nothing are reported once, as a count. Sending a job back EXPORT and choose \"DEXPI plant model\". Each line number becomes a piping network system, each drawn piece of line a segment with its centre line, equipment gets a nozzle at every connection, and the valve's actuator is written inside the valve. The class names follow the DEXPI test cases; the file is for exchange, not an engineering approval."},{"slug":"pid-not-built-yet","scope":"addon:pid","title":"P&ID: not built yet","summary":"What the P&ID add-on does not do yet, so you know before you plan a job around it.","section":"reference","order":90,"keywords":["limits","not built","missing","roadmap","p&id"],"headings":[{"id":"not-built-yet","text":"Not built yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Ch2 id=\"not-built-yet\"\u003ENot built yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESpecifications and sizes from a pipe spec\u003C/strong\u003E: a line number&#039;s size and spec are text; nothing checks them against a piping class. The 3D piping with specs is a separate add-on in BimCraft.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELoop diagrams, cause-and-effect and data sheets\u003C/strong\u003E from the instrument index.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESeveral sheets with a drawing list\u003C/strong\u003E and cross-references between off-page connectors: the connectors carry a label; nothing joins them across drawings yet.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBubbles for sub-systems, insulation and heat tracing marks\u003C/strong\u003E: insulation is a text field in the line list.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMoving a line when an item it ends on moves\u003C/strong\u003E: lines are never attached. Move the line&#039;s end with its grip.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ESymbols from other libraries\u003C/strong\u003E: the library is CadCraft&#039;s own; draw others as blocks.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EOther languages\u003C/strong\u003E for the add-on&#039;s words: they come with the next language update.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EStream splitting and optimising the temperatures\u003C/strong\u003E in the heat-exchanger network grid; the grid is a first design.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EThe PDF of the HAZOP worksheet\u003C/strong\u003E has no logo or signature block; the worksheet&#039;s PDF is plain tables.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EMarkupCraft shows the node boxes only after its own importer\u003C/strong\u003E reads the JSON file: it is exported here, not yet read there.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003ELive values from Studio\u003C/strong\u003E need Studio&#039;s plant connection (OPC UA is Rust, built with Studio).\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"Not built yet Specifications and sizes from a pipe spec: a line number's size and spec are text; nothing checks them against a piping class. The 3D piping with specs is a separate add-on in BimCraft. Loop diagrams, cause-and-effect and data sheets from the instrument index. Several sheets with a drawing list and cross-references between off-page connectors: the connectors carry a label; nothing joins them across drawings yet. Bubbles for sub-systems, insulation and heat tracing marks: insulation is a text field in the line list. Moving a line when an item it ends on moves: lines are never attached. Move the line's end with its grip. Symbols from other libraries: the library is CadCraft's own; draw others as blocks. Other languages for the add-on's words: they come with the next language update. Stream splitting and optimising the temperatures in the heat-exchanger network grid; the grid is a first design. The PDF of the HAZOP worksheet has no logo or signature block; the worksheet's PDF is plain tables. MarkupCraft shows the node boxes only after its own importer reads the JSON file: it is exported here, not yet read there. Live values from Studio need Studio's plant connection (OPC UA is Rust, built with Studio)."}]};
